**Summary of the lesson at `courses/opencv-w-wizji-komputerowej-od-podstaw-do-sar/lessons/fft-2d-i-widmo-amplitudy.json`:**
- **5 sections** (within the 4–8 range), 15 minutes, balanced theory/practice
  1. **Theory — "Po co domena częstotliwości?"** — motivation, low/high frequency intuition, with a Wikimedia inline image of Fourier synthesis
  2. **Theory — "DFT 2D, widmo amplitudy i fftshift"** — full 2D DFT formula in KaTeX, magnitude/phase, why log-scale, why `fftshift`, Hermitian symmetry — with inline Cooley–Tukey diagram
  3. **Code — `amplitude_spectrum(img)`** — implements `log1p(|fftshift(fft2(img))|)`; 4 tests (1 visible smoke + 3 hidden: shape/dtype, DC-at-centre, log(1 + sum) at DC, point symmetry of real-image spectrum); full solution
  4. **Quiz** — distractors drawn from common misconceptions (DC dominance, image size effects, `np.real` vs `np.abs`), with explanation justifying each wrong answer
  5. **Sandbox** — sinusoidal grating generator; learner varies `(kx, ky)` and watches the symmetric peak pair migrate
- **4 lesson-level sources**: Wikipedia FFT (article), OpenCV FFT tutorial (article), Cooley & Tukey 1965 (paper, with author + year), Gonzalez & Woods Ch. 4 (book, with author + year). Theory sections also carry section-level sources for the specific reference each leans on.
- All Polish content; code comments without diacritics (matching the existing course convention); image URLs from Wikimedia Commons with descriptive alt text and brief Polish attribution.
- No files under `scripts/ralph/`, `course.json`, or other lesson files were touched.
